<br>information-kalman-linter <a data-href="SKILL" href="information-kalman-linter/skill.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">SKILL</a>
<br>Kalman-reviewer <a data-href="kalman-reviewer" href="dot_gemini/agents/kalman-reviewer.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">kalman-reviewer</a> 1.1 Limitations of the Standard Kalman Filter: Review of the "Whiteness" and "Independence" assumptions. 1.2 The Concept of State Internalization: Moving external stochastic processes into the state vector. 1.3 Dimensionality vs. Optimality: Understanding the computational trade-offs () when inflating the state. 2.1 Physical Sources of Coupling: Accelerometers and force sensors in dynamic assemblies. 2.2 The "Control Form" Timing Issue: Why and correlate in discrete-time systems. 2.3 Mathematical Restructuring: Using and to decorrelate future noise from current measurements. 3.1 Input Dependency: How noisy inputs in and in contaminate state estimates. 3.2 Temporal Dependency: Solving the correlation between the previous estimate and the current input . 3.3 Simultaneous Augmentation: Handling coupled noise and noisy inputs in a unified framework. 4.1 Sources of Color: Sensor drift, ground irregularities, and actuator lags. 4.2 Gauss-Markov Processes: Modeling Random Walks and Autoregressive (AR) noise. 4.3 Higher-Order Shaping Filters: * 2nd-Order: Modeling structural resonance and periodic vibrations. 4th-Order: Implementing Butterworth profiles for pre-filtered sensor inputs. 4.4 Aliasing as Correlation: Modeling the "ghost" frequencies of undersampled high-frequency noise. 5.1 Unknown Cross-Correlation: Dealing with shared histories between separate filters (Covariance Intersection). 5.2 Circular Correlation: Data rumoring in sensor networks and information de-correlation. 5.3 State-Parameter Correlation: Introduction to Joint Estimation (EKF/UKF) for uncertain mass/stiffness parameters. 6.1 The Whiteness Test: Autocorrelation of Innovations as the "Gold Standard" for filter optimality. 6.2 Statistical Criteria: Using 95% Confidence Bounds () to quantify performance. 6.3 Failure Diagnosis: Interpreting Autocorrelation Function (ACF) patterns to identify unmodeled dynamics. MATLAB Exercise: Design an augmented Kalman Filter for a flexible link system subject to ground irregularities and a Butterworth-filtered control input. Verification: Perform an ACF test on residuals to prove that the augmented state has successfully "whitened" the innovations.
<br><a data-href="Handling Correlated Process and Measurement Noise via State Augmentation" href="handling-correlated-process-and-measurement-noise-via-state-augmentation.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Handling Correlated Process and Measurement Noise via State Augmentation</a><br><a data-href="Handling Noisy Inputs via State Augmentation" href="handling-noisy-inputs-via-state-augmentation.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Handling Noisy Inputs via State Augmentation</a><br><a data-href="Handling Simultaneous Noise Correlation and Input Noise Handling" href="handling-simultaneous-noise-correlation-and-input-noise-handling.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Handling Simultaneous Noise Correlation and Input Noise Handling</a>While we have covered the primary "structural" correlation problems solvable through state augmentation—such as coupled process/measurement noise, noisy inputs, and temporal "coloring"—there are a few remaining correlation-related non-idealities in the linear Gaussian framework that require distinct mathematical strategies.<br><a data-href="Handling Sources of Temporal Correlation (Colored Noise)" href="handling-sources-of-temporal-correlation-(colored-noise).html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Handling Sources of Temporal Correlation (Colored Noise)</a>A significant problem in multi-sensor systems or decentralized estimation is the Unknown Cross-Correlation between two different filters.
The Source: If two separate Kalman Filters are estimating the same state (e.g., two robots sharing data), their estimates and become correlated because they likely share a common process noise "history." The Challenge: Unlike the previous cases, we often don't know the exact value of the cross-covariance . If we treat them as independent, the filter becomes overconfident and may diverge. The Solution: This is typically handled not by state augmentation, but by Covariance Intersection (CI). CI provides a way to fuse two estimates by finding a "bounding" covariance that is guaranteed to be consistent even when the correlation between the estimates is unknown. In complex multibody networks (like a swarm of sensors), information can loop back to its source.
The Source: Sensor A gives data to Sensor B, which then passes a processed estimate back to Sensor A. The Problem: Sensor A perceives its own original information as "new" independent evidence. This positive feedback loop (or "rumoring") artificially shrinks the covariance . The Solution: This is usually managed via Information Filters or Track-to-Track Fusion algorithms that use "Information De-correlation" to subtract out common historical information before performing an update. In multibody dynamics, you often have parameters (like a mass or a stiffness ) that are uncertain.
The Source: The "noise" in the estimate of is correlated with the state because dictates how affects the system. The Solution: This is the bridge to the Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) or Dual Kalman Filter. While you do augment the state with the parameters (), the problem becomes inherently nonlinear because the state and parameters are multiplied together in the dynamics (). The standard linear augmentation we discussed earlier is insufficient because the transition matrix itself becomes a function of the augmented state. For your students, a great way to conclude this chapter is to teach them the Whiteness Test of Residuals (Innovations).Regardless of which correlation problem they are solving, the "proof" that they have successfully handled the correlation is that the resulting innovation sequence must be pure white noise. If they perform an autocorrelation on their residuals and find a peak at any lag other than zero, there is a correlation problem they haven't modeled yet—be it aliasing, resonance, or a noisy input.The Whiteness Test of Residuals is the definitive "diagnostic" for any Kalman Filter implementation. In an optimal filter, the innovation (the difference between the actual measurement and the predicted measurement) should contain no information. If information remains, it manifests as temporal correlation, meaning your state augmentation hasn't fully captured the system's "color."The innovation at time is defined as:If the filter is optimal, the sequence must be a zero-mean white noise process. This implies that the Autocorrelation Function (ACF) of the residuals should be zero for all lags .To quantify this for your students, we use the 95% Confidence Bounds.For a residual sequence of length , the autocorrelation coefficients for should fall within the following range:
Criterion: If more than 5% of the lag coefficients (outside of lag 0) exceed these bounds, the null hypothesis that the noise is white is rejected. Implication: If the ACF shows a "sinusoidal" pattern, you have unmodeled vibrations (resonance). If it shows a "decaying exponential," you have unmodeled colored noise or bias. This script simulates a residual sequence and performs the whiteness test using the built-in autocorrelation function.Matlab% Whiteness Test for Kalman Filter Innovations
N = 1000; % Number of samples
fs = 100; % Sampling frequency (Hz)
t = (0:N-1)/fs; % Case A: Optimal Residuals (White Noise)
d_white = randn(N, 1); % Case B: Suboptimal Residuals (Aliasing or Unmodeled Resonance at 5Hz)
d_correlated = randn(N, 1) + 0.5*sin(2*pi*5*t)'; % Perform Autocorrelation
[acf_w, lags] = autocorr(d_white, 'NumLags', 50);
[acf_c, ~] = autocorr(d_correlated, 'NumLags', 50); % Define 95% Confidence Bound
bound = 1.96 / sqrt(N); % Plotting results for students
figure;
subplot(2,1,1);
stem(lags, acf_w, 'filled'); hold on;
yline([bound, -bound], 'r--', '95% Confidence');
title('Innovation ACF: Optimal Filter (White)');
grid on; subplot(2,1,2);
stem(lags, acf_c, 'filled'); hold on;
yline([bound, -bound], 'r--', '95% Confidence');
title('Innovation ACF: Suboptimal Filter (Correlated)');
grid on; % Quantification logic
out_of_bounds = sum(abs(acf_c(2:end)) &gt; bound);
if out_of_bounds &gt; (0.05 * 50) fprintf('Result: Residuals are NOT white. Check for unmodeled dynamics.\n');
else fprintf('Result: Residuals are white. Filter is optimal.\n');
end
For a student analyzing their multibody dynamics simulation: Run the Filter: Collect the innovation sequence over a significant time window. Compute ACF: Use autocorr in MATLAB. Check Bounds: If the spikes remain inside the lines, the state augmentation is successful. Diagnose: * Large spikes at low lags: Unmodeled slow drift (e.g., ground irregularities). Periodic spikes: Unmodeled periodic vibrations or aliasing. This test is the final "grade" of a Kalman Filter's performance. If the residuals are white, no further state augmentation can improve the estimate.
